        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe membership-level-cap logic itself is placed reasonably (schema columns, entity field, service derivation, mapper capping all mirror the existing `householdMemberCount` / membership-point patterns). The problem is what this change leaves behind.\n\n**1. `sharesHousehold` is now dead code threaded through three layers (primary finding).**\nThe removed `rejectWhenHouseholdDuplicate(owner, householdMembers, sharesHousehold)` call was the *only* consumer of the `sharesHousehold` parameter. After this diff:\n- `ClinicServiceImpl.createOwner(Owner, boolean sharesHousehold)` (line 263) never references `sharesHousehold` in its body \u2014 the boolean is inert.\n- The two-arg overload is now behaviorally identical to the one-arg `createOwner(Owner)`, which still delegates with `createOwner(owner, false)` (line 258) as if the flag mattered.\n- `OwnerRestControllerV1` (line 140) still computes and passes `Boolean.TRUE.equals(ownerFieldsDto.getSharesHousehold())` into a parameter that controls nothing, and the OpenAPI schema still documents `sharesHousehold` as a meaningful write-only field.\n\nA maintainer reading this will reasonably assume the flag still gates something. Either remove the now-vestigial parameter/overload and its controller plumbing (and reconcile the OpenAPI contract), or, if the flag is meant to be retained for the new capping behavior, wire it in explicitly. Leaving a silently-ignored boolean spanning controller \u2192 interface \u2192 impl is the kind of incoherence this review targets.\n\n**2. Service-layer dependency on the DTO mapper is a mild layering wrinkle.**\n`ClinicServiceImpl` now injects `OwnerMapper` purely to call `ownerMapper::membershipLevel` (line 356) \u2014 reaching into a MapStruct entity\u2194DTO presentation component to perform a domain calculation during a business operation. Reusing `membershipLevel` does avoid duplicating the level-derivation ladder, so this is defensible, but the natural home for a business rule (\"membership level of an owner\") is the domain/service side, with the mapper delegating to it rather than the reverse. If the level derivation is genuinely business logic, consider hoisting it so both the service and the mapper depend on a domain method, rather than the service depending on the mapper. Not blocking on its own, but worth a look while addressing finding 1."
